Hi Llu?s, Robert,

I can't speak for whether org.Hs.eg.db will get its PATH map back, but the
NA values come from KEGG's restrictions on redistribution, so I wouldn't
count on it. If what you need is a gene-to-pathway map for examples or
tests, an openly licensed one is easy to get, and I can offer one route.

I've been building bioc-on-ice, which republishes public annotation (NCBI
Gene, GO, Ensembl, HGNC, WikiPathways, and others) as Parquet files plus a
small DuckDB file of views. There's no account and nothing to install
beyond the duckdb R package. Each release sits in a dated folder, so a
vignette can pin one and get the same answer every time. Details and
release notes are here:
https://bioconice.cancerdatasci.org/releases?utm_source=bioc-devel&utm_medium=email&utm_campaign=org-hs-eg-db-kegg

KEGG is not in it, for the same licensing reason. WikiPathways is (CC0,
refreshed monthly): 991 human pathways covering 9,031 Entrez genes in the
current release. Reactome (also CC0) is next on the list.

1. A drop-in for as.list(org.Hs.egPATH)
---------------------------------------

library(DBI)
con <- dbConnect(duckdb::duckdb())
dbExecute(con, "INSTALL httpfs; LOAD httpfs;")
dbExecute(con, "ATTACH '
https://data.bioconice.cancerdatasci.org/v2026-11-08/bioconice.duckdb'
                AS bioc (READ_ONLY)")

gp <- dbGetQuery(con, "
  SELECT gp.gene_id, gp.pathway_id, p.name
  FROM bioc.annotation.wikipathways__gene_pathway gp
  JOIN bioc.annotation.wikipathways__pathway p USING (pathway_id)
  WHERE gp.taxon_id = 9606")
dbDisconnect(con, shutdown = TRUE)

# Same shape as as.list(org.Hs.egPATH): Entrez gene ID -> pathway IDs
egPATH <- split(gp$pathway_id, gp$gene_id)
egPATH[["7157"]]                      # TP53: WP710, WP4754, WP615, ...

# Pathway ID -> name
pathway_names <- unique(gp[, c("pathway_id", "name")])

# Gene sets the way most enrichment tools take them
term2gene <- gp[, c("pathway_id", "gene_id")]       #
clusterProfiler::enricher(TERM2GENE = )
pathways  <- split(gp$gene_id, gp$pathway_id)       # fgsea(pathways = )

A named list covers what code usually does with org.Hs.egPATH:
egPATH[keys] replaces mget(keys, org.Hs.egPATH), and lengths(egPATH)
counts pathways per gene. If something really needs an
environment, list2env(egPATH) gives you one.

One caveat: these are different pathways from KEGG's, with different gene
sets. Any enrichment results shown in a vignette will change, not just the
IDs.

The "v2026-11-08" in the URL is the pin. Use "latest" instead to track the
newest release. That host keeps weekly releases for about a month. For
something that has to build for years, like a vignette, use the monthly
archive on Hugging Face instead: each month's release is tagged YYYY-MM
there, and tags are never deleted. The two WikiPathways files are about
360 KB together, so download them rather than querying in place
(anonymous Hugging Face requests are rate-limited):

base <- "
https://huggingface.co/datasets/seandavis/bioconice/resolve/2026-11/annotation/
"
for (f in c("wikipathways__gene_pathway.parquet",
"wikipathways__pathway.parquet"))
  download.file(paste0(base, f), f, mode = "wb")

con <- dbConnect(duckdb::duckdb())
gp <- dbGetQuery(con, "
  SELECT gp.gene_id, gp.pathway_id, p.name
  FROM 'wikipathways__gene_pathway.parquet' gp
  JOIN 'wikipathways__pathway.parquet' p USING (pathway_id)
  WHERE gp.taxon_id = 9606")
dbDisconnect(con, shutdown = TRUE)
egPATH <- split(gp$pathway_id, gp$gene_id)

The 2026-11 tag is release 2026-11-01. Its manifest.json lists every
source version that went into it, if you want to cite exactly what you
used. The whole release is browsable at
https://huggingface.co/datasets/seandavis/bioconice?utm_source=bioc-devel&utm_medium=email&utm_campaign=org-hs-eg-db-kegg

2. Rolling your own OrgDb with a pathway column
-----------------------------------------------

If you'd rather keep the select()/mapIds() interface, AnnotationForge can
build an org package straight from the same tables. This builds and
installs (I tested it today); the GOALL tables come from your installed
GO.db.

library(DBI)
con <- dbConnect(duckdb::duckdb())
dbExecute(con, "INSTALL httpfs; LOAD httpfs;")
dbExecute(con, "ATTACH '
https://data.bioconice.cancerdatasci.org/v2026-11-08/bioconice.duckdb'
                AS bioc (READ_ONLY)")
q <- function(sql) dbGetQuery(con, sql)

# makeOrgPackage rules: each table's first column is GID, and the other
# column names become select() columns, so they must be unique.
gene_info <- q("SELECT DISTINCT gene_id AS GID, symbol AS SYMBOL,
description AS GENENAME
                FROM bioc.annotation.ncbi__gene WHERE taxon_id = 9606")
chromosome <- q("SELECT DISTINCT gene_id AS GID, chromosome AS CHR
                 FROM bioc.annotation.ncbi__gene
                 WHERE taxon_id = 9606 AND chromosome IS NOT NULL")
go <- q("SELECT DISTINCT gene_id AS GID, go_id AS GO, evidence AS EVIDENCE
         FROM bioc.annotation.ncbi__gene_go WHERE taxon_id = 9606")
wikipathways <- q("SELECT DISTINCT gene_id AS GID, pathway_id AS
WIKIPATHWAYS
                   FROM bioc.annotation.wikipathways__gene_pathway WHERE
taxon_id = 9606")
xref <- function(ns) q(sprintf(
  "SELECT DISTINCT source_id AS GID, target_id AS %s
   FROM bioc.annotation.identifier_mapping
   WHERE taxon_id = 9606 AND source_namespace = 'ENTREZ' AND
target_namespace = '%s'",
  ns, ns))
alias <- xref("ALIAS"); ensembl <- xref("ENSEMBL")
uniprot <- xref("UNIPROT"); omim <- xref("OMIM")
dbDisconnect(con, shutdown = TRUE)   # run every query before this line

AnnotationForge::makeOrgPackage(
  gene_info = gene_info, chromosome = chromosome, go = go,
  alias = alias, ensembl = ensembl, uniprot = uniprot, omim = omim,
  wikipathways = wikipathways,
  version = "0.0.1", maintainer = "You <you at example.org>", author = "You",
  outputDir = ".", tax_id = "9606", genus = "Homo", species = "sapiens",
  goTable = "go")

install.packages("org.Hsapiens.eg.db", repos = NULL, type = "source")
library(org.Hsapiens.eg.db)
select(org.Hsapiens.eg.db, keys = "TP53", keytype = "SYMBOL",
       columns = c("GID", "WIKIPATHWAYS"))

# The org.Hs.egPATH-style list, from the OrgDb
egPATH <- mapIds(org.Hsapiens.eg.db, keys(org.Hsapiens.eg.db, "GID"),
                 column = "WIKIPATHWAYS", keytype = "GID", multiVals =
"list")
egPATH <- egPATH[!vapply(egPATH, function(v) all(is.na(v)), TRUE)]

Things that tripped me up:

- The key column is GID, not ENTREZID. That's how makeOrgPackage names it,
  so code that hardcodes keytype = "ENTREZID" needs a change.
- Packages built this way have the OrgDb object only, no org.Xx.egPATH
  style bimaps. Those come only with the official org packages. mapIds()
  as above gives you the same list.
- The installed package is about 750 MB, almost all of it the GOALL
  tables. Drop go/goTable if you only need pathways.
- GOALL is computed from your local GO.db. If that GO release differs from
  the one NCBI's gene2go used, some terms can drop out of the ancestor
  closure.

If you need KEGG itself and you're fine with KEGG's terms, KEGGREST will
fetch it at run time. For a vignette that has to build on the build
machines, I'd avoid the network dependency.

Happy to help if either of you tries this and hits a snag.

Sean

